# a-maze-and-a-core — plan 11 F5.
#
# The scenario where the enemies ROUTE instead of drifting: a corridor
# at the core, walled shut, with a two-hex bypass around the block.
#
# ── What makes this one evidence ────────────────────────────────
#
# Every earlier enemy scenario walks a ONE-HEX-WIDE corridor, and on
# those a flow field and a fixed heading produce the identical path —
# so they could not tell the two apart.
#
# This one can, exactly.  Every spawn heading here is 4, which is
# (-1, 0): it changes q and NEVER r.  So `enemy 0 3 -1` below is a
# hex no heading could have reached, and it is the whole gate.  Before
# F5 these four stopped dead at (4, 0) in front of the wall and stayed
# there; a run of this file was red at that line.
#
# ── The world ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   r = 0 :  (0,0) … (12,0)     the corridor, core at one end
#   (3,0) :  WALL               the direct way in, shut
#   (4,-1), (3,-1)              the bypass, two hexes round it
#
# 13 steps by the bypass where the direct line would have been 12.

echo a maze and a core: the wall is shut, so they go round

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn, twelve hexes out — outside close_spawn_disable_radius
# (10), so the wave actually emits — facing back down the corridor.
# ⚠ TWICE: the place-kind cycle is spawn -> target -> tower -> spawn
# since plan 12 B5a gave towers a marker kind of their own.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 12 0
marker 12 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

# The corridor, then the bypass, then the wall that makes the bypass
# the only way in.
palette 5
drag 12 0 0 0
click 3 -1
click 2 -1
palette 9
click 3 0

count painted 15 15
kind 3 0 wall
kind 2 -1 grass
kind 3 -1 grass

wave 4
count alive 4 4
enemy 0 12 0
enemies passable
range 12 12
snap maze-wave-starts

# ⚠ **The pre-walk window** (plan 16 W2): 8 ticks standing at the
# marker, and re-asserting the position is what GATES it.
tick 8
enemy 0 12 0
range 12 12

# Eight ticks: down the corridor to the hex in front of the wall.
# This is where every pre-F5 run stopped for good.  The other three
# are behind it at 5, 6 and 7 — they left the spawn one per tick,
# because a companion blocks a step (plan 11 F5c).
tick 8
enemy 0 4 0
enemy 3 7 0
enemies passable
enemies distinct
range 4 7

# Two more, and they are OFF the line their heading could reach.
# (3, -1) has r = -1; a heading of (-1, 0) cannot produce that hex,
# so this line is the routing, stated as a coordinate.
tick 2
enemy 0 2 -1
enemies passable
enemies distinct
range 3 5
snap maze-round-the-wall

# Three more and the leader is home.  13 steps for a 12-hex corridor:
# the detour costs exactly the one extra step the bypass adds.
#
# ⚠ The other three do NOT pile onto the core hex behind it.  Each is
# one tick behind the one in front, so the wave arrives as a column —
# and `enemy 3 3 -1` is the tail still on the bypass while the leader
# is home.  Before F5c all four read (0, 0).
tick 3
enemy 0 0 0
enemy 3 2 -1
enemies passable
enemies distinct
range 0 3
count alive 4 4
